/-!
# Finiteness of primitive characters of bounded conductor

Theorem statement: `HorizontalPadicL.primitiveCharacters_boundedConductor_finite_v2`
(`e6093102-59af-48b8-afd3-a5d02429e9b9`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-25.

Proof: submission `253df150-bf20-46af-8fb7-512ff86ef721`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-25 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

There are only finitely many primitive algebraic Dirichlet characters
with conductor bounded by a fixed real number. Primitivity prevents duplicate
presentations at unbounded levels.

## Explanation of the source proof

A primitive character is represented at its conductor. Under a fixed real conductor bound there are
only finitely many possible positive integer conductors, and for each conductor the corresponding
unit group is finite. Hence there are only finitely many homomorphisms from these unit groups to the
finite-order elements of the algebraic closure, proving finiteness of the bounded-conductor set.

